# Keep line endings LF in the working tree, not just in the repo.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true on Windows rewrites files to CRLF on checkout, and
# prettier (endOfLine: "lf", the default) then fails format:check on files nobody edited.
# That happened: a single `git checkout -- package.json` was enough to break `pnpm verify`
# locally while CI, which checks out on Linux, stayed green.
#
# That line used to end "no tracked file in this repo is binary, so text=auto is unambiguous
# here." The app icons made it false: `deno task icons` writes six PNGs and two `.ico` files, and
# they are committed.
#
# `text=auto` would in fact have handled them — git sniffs for a NUL in the first 8000 bytes and
# leaves anything it finds one in alone, and every PNG has one in its signature. The explicit
# `binary` lines below are not there because that heuristic is wrong; they are there because it is
# a heuristic, and the failure mode when it misses is a `.ico` with every 0x0a in it rewritten to
# 0x0d0a — a file that still looks plausible in `git status` and decodes nowhere.
#
# `binary` is git's own shorthand for `-text -diff`: never convert line endings, and do not try to
# show a textual diff of it.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
*.ico binary
